\chapter{Structural records and sums}
\label{ch:21}
\index{Structural records and sums}

Structural records name their fields, while structural sums name possible constructors and their payload types. A record value always supplies the fields in its declared shape. A sum value selects one constructor, and payload access needs evidence of that tag. Fixed layouts enable the C11 emitter to construct ordinary records and discriminated values. The language still checks field names and types before choosing that layout.

\section{Modeling an outcome}
Represent a successful count and a failed input as distinct cases so downstream code handles them explicitly. A Boolean paired with a number leaves readers to remember when the number is meaningful. A sum expresses that rule in the shape and supports pattern matching on the active case. For a structural record, reorder the fields in a construction and compare the projection results. For a sum, cover both cases through \OnxCodeTerm{match} and inspect the emitted tag test. Named records and enums can additionally preserve declaration identity, as \cref{ch:23} explains.

\section{Separate label order from evaluation order}
In the data-model example the function called magnitude returns the sum
of the squares, not the square root. Passing x equal to three and y
equal to four gives 25. Exchanging the order of the labeled initializers
does not exchange the coordinates. The type checker compares labeled
shapes, and the compiler can arrange their fields in canonical order.
In fact the sum of squares alone would make a poor test of this last
point: actually swapping x and y would still give 25. To test projection,
read x and y separately and expect three and four respectively.

There is one more order to test. Replace the two constants with calls
that print their argument before returning it as a coordinate. Write
the initializer for y first. Its print should come first even if the
generated layout puts x first. A backend must not sort the calls merely
because it sorts the labels.

Now wrap the point in the Point case and pass it to the classifier.
The selected arm projects the record and gives the same 25. The None
case instead carries an empty record and returns zero without reading
coordinates. An origin with both coordinates zero would also score zero,
but it would be a different sum value: a consumer wishing to distinguish
``no measurement'' from ``measured zero'' should inspect the constructor
before collapsing them to one number.

